= Pragmatics — Technical =

== Boundary problem ==
There is no universally accepted semantics–pragmatics boundary.

Positions differ over whether context sensitivity is lexically triggered, compositionally represented, pragmatically supplied, or distributed across multiple representational levels.

A theory may distinguish:

<pre>
character / linguistic rule
       ↓ context
content / proposition
       ↓ pragmatic reasoning
communicated content / act
</pre>

Other frameworks reject or reorganize these layers.

== Context models ==
Formal pragmatics may represent context through:

* indexical parameters such as speaker, time, place and world;
* sets of possible worlds;
* information states;
* discourse referents;
* commitment stores;
* Questions Under Discussion;
* probabilistic beliefs over intentions or interpretations.

These are modeling choices, not equivalent definitions of context.

== Gricean inference ==
In a Gricean framework, hearers reason about a cooperative speaker's intended contribution relative to conversational expectations.

Conversational implicatures are defeasible and typically sensitive to alternatives and context.

Modern pragmatic theories include neo-Gricean, relevance-theoretic, dynamic, game-theoretic, probabilistic, interactional and sociopragmatic approaches.

== Interface phenomena ==
Presupposition projection, anaphora, ellipsis, scalar implicature, irony, metaphor, indirect speech acts, politeness and information structure test the division between grammar, semantics, inference and discourse.

